chcp: fix inverted DEVICE/CNO argument detection

The [DEVICE] argument in chcp's own usage string cannot work: a device path
fails nilfs_parse_cno() and is left in the checkpoint list ("invalid checkpoint
number: /dev/foo"), while a numeric first-of-two argument is taken as the device
("cannot open NILFS on 123"). rmcp.c has the same construct written correctly.

Without a usable device argument chcp falls back to nilfs_open(NULL, ...), which
takes the first rw nilfs2 line in /proc/mounts, so on a host with more than one
NILFS2 filesystem it applies one filesystem's checkpoint numbers to another.

--- a/bin/chcp.c
+++ b/bin/chcp.c
@@ -118,9 +118,9 @@
 		modestr = argv[optind++];
 		cno = nilfs_parse_cno(argv[optind], &endptr, CHCP_BASE);
 		if (cno >= NILFS_CNO_MAX || *endptr != '\0')
-			dev = NULL;
-		else
 			dev = argv[optind++];
+		else
+			dev = NULL;
 	}
 
 	if (strcmp(modestr, CHCP_MODE_CP) == 0)
